<!--
  AppStream metadata for the Flathub listing.

  This file is the listing. It is what a user reads in GNOME Software or KDE Discover
  before deciding to install, and it is indexed as a web page on flathub.org — so the
  limitations below belong here rather than only in the README. A Flatpak that cannot
  type into other windows, on a user who never read the caveat, is a one-star review.
-->
<component type="desktop-application">
  <id>com.mskazemi.YazSes</id>
  <name>YazSes</name>
  <summary>Offline voice dictation that types into the focused app</summary>

  <metadata_license>CC0-1.0</metadata_license>
  <project_license>Apache-2.0</project_license>

  <developer id="com.mskazemi">
    <name>Mohsen Seyedkazemi Ardebili</name>
  </developer>

  <description>
    <p>
      YazSes is offline voice dictation. Hold a key, speak, release — your words are
      transcribed on your own CPU and typed into whatever window has focus: editor,
      browser, terminal or chat.
    </p>
    <p>
      After installation and a one-time model download, speech recognition runs locally.
      Audio and transcripts are not sent to any cloud service, and there is no account,
      API key, GPU or subscription.
    </p>
    <p>It also works on recordings:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Transcribe an audio or video file to text, SRT, WebVTT, Markdown or JSON</li>
      <li>Label who said what, using speaker models that run on your machine</li>
      <li>Capture a whole meeting hands-free and get a speaker-labelled transcript</li>
      <li>Optionally write minutes with a local language model you supply</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Known limitations, so they are not a surprise after installing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The first run downloads a speech model (a few hundred megabytes) and needs a
        network connection. Transcription itself works with networking disabled.
      </li>
      <li>
        The shipped models are tuned for English. Other languages need a multilingual
        model selected explicitly, and accuracy varies.
      </li>
      <li>
        Hold-to-talk needs permission to read keyboard events. If your system declines
        that permission, the daemon starts and looks healthy but never hears the key.
      </li>
      <li>
        Typing into other applications depends on the sandbox permissions granted to this
        build. Where injection is not permitted, dictated text is placed on the clipboard
        instead, and you paste it yourself.
      </li>
      <li>Desktop only — there is no mobile version.</li>
    </ul>
  </description>

  <launchable type="desktop-id">com.mskazemi.YazSes.desktop</launchable>

  <!--
    Screenshots are not decoration on Flathub: they are the listing. GNOME Software
    and KDE Discover render this block as the app's entire visual identity, and
    flathub's linter flags a desktop-application that has none. This file had no
    <screenshots> at all, which is a second submission blocker alongside the demo
    video the checklist asks for (issue #45).

    URLs point at `main` rather than a tag on purpose. Flathub downloads and mirrors
    these at build time, so what ships is a snapshot either way — but a tag pin would
    keep serving an old UI after a redesign, and would silently 404 the first time a
    screenshot is renamed.

    Captions are one sentence with no trailing full stop, which is Flathub's stated
    style rule. Flathub sets no minimum width; it asks that the captured window be
    1000x700 or smaller (2000x1400 HiDPI). The 620 px floor these images clear is
    ours, not theirs — see tests/test_flatpak_metainfo.py.
  -->
